[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [[:db/retract 17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189761] [:db/retract 17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189762] [:db/retract 17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189763] [:db/retract 17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189764] [:db/retract 17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189765] {:db/id 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" "question-851fa16f-b92a-5672-9bbf-979f258829aa" "question-24ed500c-c9f9-51ed-ab79-e8e54e985d91" "question-812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192387 :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7  x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=2.4,$ the mean square error of the model is $1.25,$ and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg) }\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 1.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.90=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.25 \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{8} + \\dfrac{2.4^2}{42} \\bigg)} \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  1.6 \\pm 1.1\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d" "question-14b7fe25-0e56-53c7-82f0-cba7c7a1a3e7" "question-a77fcdca-ce6d-5371-a7a2-e886bf8d2462" "question-4f56dfe4-956b-5759-bcb7-ccce82fe1dbd" "question-8c255cc5-a4ec-5d1f-be58-dbb45c0a4d57"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192388 :question/problem "| **Hours ($x$)** | **$4$** | **$4.5$** | **$5.5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grades ($y$)** | $62$ | $65$ | $77$ | $81$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours each of four students spent studying for a biology exam and the grades they obtained on the exam. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=5,$ and $\\textrm{MSE}=1.375,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg) }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x= 21.3 + 10x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\beta_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=21.3 + 10x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 21.3.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.005} = 2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing $S_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(4-5)^{2}+(4.5-5)^{2}+(5.5-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0.25+0.25+1 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 2.920 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.375\\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{5^2}{2.5} \\bigg)} \\approx 11.0\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  21.3 \\pm 11.0\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" :question/id #uuid "f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159563" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{10},{y}_{10})$ of $n=10$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2.7,$ the mean square error of the model is $3.6,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=82.5,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.8-3.3x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{2.7}^{2}}{82.5})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.8\\pm 2.8\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/id #uuid "08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer-field/choices ["answer-25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90" "answer-868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c" "answer-460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7" "answer-b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a" "answer-e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90" :answer/id #uuid "25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8\\pm 1.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c" :answer/id #uuid "868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8\\pm 1.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7" :answer/id #uuid "460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a" :answer/id #uuid "b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer/id #uuid "e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192979 :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{20},{y}_{20})$ of $n=20$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2,$ the mean square error of the model is $8,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=24,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.101)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $18$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.5+5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=3.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.101)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{18,0.025}=2.101.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.101\\cdot \\sqrt{8\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{24})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.5\\pm 2.8\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189760 :answer-field/correct "answer-a9cd2e1a-8327-5ae8-8f04-0b0a204554bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a18bd7cb-4599-5047-a357-7726837ec776" "answer-a9cd2e1a-8327-5ae8-8f04-0b0a204554bc" "answer-3b4ae633-d836-59a7-8030-002829769a32" "answer-f48cd6d6-78c5-50fe-84ac-c81348f46ac9" "answer-ede2b151-7a0f-5a99-a1ae-0b5aa02441f5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a18bd7cb-4599-5047-a357-7726837ec776" :answer/id #uuid "a18bd7cb-4599-5047-a357-7726837ec776" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.5\\pm 1.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9cd2e1a-8327-5ae8-8f04-0b0a204554bc" :answer/id #uuid "a9cd2e1a-8327-5ae8-8f04-0b0a204554bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.5\\pm 2.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b4ae633-d836-59a7-8030-002829769a32" :answer/id #uuid "3b4ae633-d836-59a7-8030-002829769a32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.5\\pm 3.0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f48cd6d6-78c5-50fe-84ac-c81348f46ac9" :answer/id #uuid "f48cd6d6-78c5-50fe-84ac-c81348f46ac9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pm 1.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ede2b151-7a0f-5a99-a1ae-0b5aa02441f5" :answer/id #uuid "ede2b151-7a0f-5a99-a1ae-0b5aa02441f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pm 2.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-851fa16f-b92a-5672-9bbf-979f258829aa" :question/id #uuid "851fa16f-b92a-5672-9bbf-979f258829aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-159560" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=5+0.4x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{10},{y}_{10})$ of $n=10$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=4.3,$ the mean square error of the model is $3.7,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=330,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=5+0.4x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=5+0.4x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{4.3}^{2}}{330})}≈2.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=5\\pm 2.5\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-caf088fc-e014-5a09-8ceb-3ba512fc68e7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-caf088fc-e014-5a09-8ceb-3ba512fc68e7" :answer-field/id #uuid "caf088fc-e014-5a09-8ceb-3ba512fc68e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3f4f31a4-eeac-52ca-b025-6e0b059772dd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd53447b-e183-5dfb-8fe3-9f2284148e07" "answer-19ea48dc-2bbd-5e4f-9e61-02d95414ac74" "answer-3f4f31a4-eeac-52ca-b025-6e0b059772dd" "answer-ce12ef9b-5ac2-5639-b917-770b6e5414d3" "answer-60c1e936-44cd-56c8-8e97-44a0822f3d4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd53447b-e183-5dfb-8fe3-9f2284148e07" :answer/id #uuid "dd53447b-e183-5dfb-8fe3-9f2284148e07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pm 2.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19ea48dc-2bbd-5e4f-9e61-02d95414ac74" :answer/id #uuid "19ea48dc-2bbd-5e4f-9e61-02d95414ac74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pm 2.3" 