t  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.6-3.9x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.6-3.9x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=12.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.921)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{16,0.005}=2.921.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.921\\cdot \\sqrt{2.1\\cdot (\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{{3.5}^{2}}{85})}≈1.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=12.6\\pm 1.9\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c654e4fd-8bc7-5442-8ca4-e6531d76e850"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c654e4fd-8bc7-5442-8ca4-e6531d76e850" :answer-field/id #uuid "c654e4fd-8bc7-5442-8ca4-e6531d76e850" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f661769d-8896-57ab-83d7-39025d585319" :answer-field/choices ["answer-402a3262-d142-567d-bf7d-c8b89a4726dc" "answer-3b96016e-6c70-5725-be4a-7c141b414f3f" "answer-83ff5ce8-83cc-52e2-94e9-f36326bca2f2" "answer-9870a91b-b9ca-50af-8384-c34539b8a4b1" "answer-f661769d-8896-57ab-83d7-39025d585319"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-402a3262-d142-567d-bf7d-c8b89a4726dc" :answer/id #uuid "402a3262-d142-567d-bf7d-c8b89a4726dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b96016e-6c70-5725-be4a-7c141b414f3f" :answer/id #uuid "3b96016e-6c70-5725-be4a-7c141b414f3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83ff5ce8-83cc-52e2-94e9-f36326bca2f2" :answer/id #uuid "83ff5ce8-83cc-52e2-94e9-f36326bca2f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9870a91b-b9ca-50af-8384-c34539b8a4b1" :answer/id #uuid "9870a91b-b9ca-50af-8384-c34539b8a4b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f661769d-8896-57ab-83d7-39025d585319" :answer/id #uuid "f661769d-8896-57ab-83d7-39025d585319" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf" :question/id #uuid "812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-159561" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=1.2,$ the mean square error of the model is $0.7,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=18.9,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.228)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.4+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.4.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.228)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{10,0.025}=2.228.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.228\\cdot \\sqrt{0.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{{1.2}^{2}}{18.9})}≈0.7\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.4\\pm 0.7\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/id #uuid "b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8c71797-2619-523f-998b-4dc9f5e6a269" "answer-b312c9b8-2b8a-552e-98f0-214625871779" "answer-93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c" "answer-489fcd75-9368-5e9b-acbf-94835400f3d5" "answer-92b0899f-5335-5dd0-a82e-12c93b11cd39"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8c71797-2619-523f-998b-4dc9f5e6a269" :answer/id #uuid "e8c71797-2619-523f-998b-4dc9f5e6a269" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b312c9b8-2b8a-552e-98f0-214625871779" :answer/id #uuid "b312c9b8-2b8a-552e-98f0-214625871779" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c" :answer/id #uuid "93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-489fcd75-9368-5e9b-acbf-94835400f3d5" :answer/id #uuid "489fcd75-9368-5e9b-acbf-94835400f3d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92b0899f-5335-5dd0-a82e-12c93b11cd39" :answer/id #uuid "92b0899f-5335-5dd0-a82e-12c93b11cd39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.4\\pm 1.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d" :question/id #uuid "69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159557" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$67$** | **$70$** | **$73$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Shoe size ($y$)** | $8.5$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and corresponding shoe sizes of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=71.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.338,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.  \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.920)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-26.6+0.5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=-26.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.920)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(67-71.5)^{2}+(70-71.5)^{2}+(73-71.5)^{2}+(76-71.5)^{2} \\\\  & =20.25+2.25+2.25+20.25 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.920\\cdot \\sqrt{0.338\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{71.5}^{2}}{45})}≈18.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=-26.6\\pm 18.1\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5f3ed31d-762f-57be-a238-32e2c1426527"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5f3ed31d-762f-57be-a238-32e2c1426527" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3ed31d-762f-57be-a238-32e2c1426527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-15060daf-a4bc-5eb8-b79b-034a85e0bbf7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e5ebf936-7757-56b0-a68f-58f3fcbd404b" "answer-e4f2c162-946e-59cc-8842-379aa299e7ac" "answer-c5f1300c-5d71-5a55-8165-193b27ab2363" "answer-15060daf-a4bc-5eb8-b79b-034a85e0bbf7" "answer-71ae2911-c3b0-54dd-b735-0b92451fc7ac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5ebf936-7757-56b0-a68f-58f3fcbd404b" :answer/id #uuid "e5ebf936-7757-56b0-a68f-58f3fcbd404b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 10.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4f2c162-946e-59cc-8842-379aa299e7ac" :answer/id #uuid "e4f2c162-946e-59cc-8842-379aa299e7ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 16.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5f1300c-5d71-5a55-8165-193b27ab2363" :answer/id #uuid "c5f1300c-5d71-5a55-8165-193b27ab2363" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 17.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15060daf-a4bc-5eb8-b79b-034a85e0bbf7" :answer/id #uuid "15060daf-a4bc-5eb8-b79b-034a85e0bbf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 18.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71ae2911-c3b0-54dd-b735-0b92451fc7ac" :answer/id #uuid "71ae2911-c3b0-54dd-b735-0b92451fc7ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 9.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-14b7fe25-0e56-53c7-82f0-cba7c7a1a3e7" :question/id #uuid "14b7fe25-0e56-53c7-82f0-cba7c7a1a3e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-159581" :question/problem "| **Number of hours ($x$)** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grade ($y$)** | $75$ | $82$ | $90$ | $95$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours four students spent studying for a math exam and the grades they obtained. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=61.7+6.8x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=3.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.9,$ find a $98%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>6.965)=0.01,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=61.7+6.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=61.7+6.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=61.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $98%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.98=0.02\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>6.965)=0.01.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.01}=6.965.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(2-3.5)^{2}+(3-3.5)^{2}+(4-3.5)^{2}+(5-3.5)^{2} \\\\  & =2.25+0.25+0.25+2.25 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=6.965\\cdot \\sqrt{0.9\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{3.5}^{2}}{5})}≈10.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=61.7\\pm 10.9\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-84de798d-98d3-5bb6-abaa-5ce3efd4cee8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-84de798d-98d3-5bb6-abaa-5ce3efd4cee8" :answer-field/id #uuid "84de798d-98d3-5bb6-abaa-5ce3efd4cee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ca5fe5a5-ce63-5f4a-900e-5dfd444db1db" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bf205baf-60e7-57ea-b105-ae0c35dd73ab" "answer-ca5fe5a5-ce63-5f4a-900e-5dfd444db1db" "answer-826dcf8a-f14a-5879-aae7-fcfae47a33b4" "answer-4a3fb017-e789-574b-a19f-04df69dcde8e" "answer-8e3c29cb-bcfa-5582-b197-0fb22fc641ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf205baf-60e7-57ea-b105-ae0c35dd73ab" :answer/id #uuid "bf205baf-60e7-57ea-b105-ae0c35dd73ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "61.7\\pm 10.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca5fe5a5-ce63-5f4a-900e-5dfd444db1db" :answer/id #uuid "ca5fe5a5-ce63-5f4a-900e-5dfd444db1db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "61.7\\pm 10.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-826dcf8a-f14a-5879-aae7-fcfae47a33b4" :answer/id #uuid "826dcf8a-f14a-5879-aae7-fcfae47a33b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "61.7\\pm 8.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a3fb017-e789-574b-a19f-04df69dcde8e" :answer/id #uuid "4a3fb017-e789-574b-a19f-04df69dcde8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "61.7\\pm 9.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e3c29cb-bcfa-5582-b197-0fb22fc641ff" :answer/id #uuid "8e3c29cb-bcfa-5582-b197-0fb22fc641ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "61.7\\pm 9.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a77fcdca-ce6d-5371-a7a2-e886bf8d2462" :question/id #uuid "a77fcdca-ce6d-5371-a7a2-e886bf8d2462" :question/math-academy-id "q-159596" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$60$** | **$62$** | **$66$** | **$68$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **IQ  ($y$)** | $110$ | $106$ | $108$ | $102$ |\n\nThe table above gives the heights, in inches, and the corresponding scores on an IQ test of four high school students. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=64,$ and $\\text{MSE}=7.7,$ find a $98%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>6.965)=0.01,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=151.3-0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=151.3.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $98%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.98=0.02\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>6.965)=0.01.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.01}=6.965.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(60-64)^{2}+(62-64)^{2}+(66-64)^{2}+(68-64)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=6.965\\cdot \\sqrt{7.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{64}^{2}}{40})}≈195.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=151.3\\pm 195.8\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b2fd0291-b761-5650-8e97-a72f4f5d21b5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b2fd0291-b761-5650-8e97-a72f4f5d21b5" :answer-field/id #uuid "b2fd0291-b761-5650-8e97-a72f4f5d21b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9f303830-c78a-5b75-886c-4c70713deba0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e489115e-e8c1-5d14-a654-2c4be9ba3e26" "answer-ac8ab9d8-4875-5fd3-8eff-2b8511dc815e" "answer-4b209ccf-fac3-51cd-a24e-17d94342e720" "answer-7e50f150-1d03-5cdd-b228-0b83996b3433" "answer-9f303830-c78a-5b75-886c-4c70713deba0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e489115e-e8c1-5d14-a654-2c4be9ba3e26" :answer/id #uuid "e489115e-e8c1-5d14-a654-2c4be9ba3e26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "151.3\\pm 173.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac8ab9d8-4875-5fd3-8eff-2b8511dc815e" :answer/id #uuid "ac8ab9d8-4875-5fd3-8eff-2b8511dc815e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "151.3\\pm 175.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b209ccf-fac3-51cd-a24e-17d94342e720" :answer/id #uuid "4b209ccf-fac3-51cd-a24e-17d94342e720" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "151.3\\pm 183.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e50f150-1d03-5cdd-b228-0b83996b3433" :answer/id #uuid "7e50f150-1d03-5cdd-b228-0b83996b3433" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "151.3\\pm 193.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f303830-c78a-5b75-886c-4c70713deba0" :answer/id #uuid "9f303830-c78a-5b75-886c-4c70713deba0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "151.3\\pm 195.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f56dfe4-956b-5759-bcb7-ccce82fe1dbd" :question/id #uuid "4f56dfe4-956b-5759-bcb7-ccce82fe1dbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-159556" :question/problem "| **Age ($x$)** | **$34$** | **$36$** | **$40$** | **$42$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Hours of sleep ($y$)** | $6.5$ | $7$ | $7.5$ | $8$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages, in years, and the number of hours each of four randomly selected adults slept last night. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=0.6+0.2x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=38,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.0125,$ find an $80%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.886)=0.1,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=0.6+0.2x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=0.6+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=0.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $80%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.8=0.2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.1.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.886)=0.1.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.1}=1.886.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(34-38)^{2}+(36-38)^{2}+(40-38)^{2}+(42-38)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=1.886\\cdot \\sqrt{0.0125\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{38}^{2}}{40})}≈1.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=0.6\\pm 1.3\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-67039e40-5de3-570b-bbed-0d3b0e5de178"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-67039e40-5de3-570b-bbed-0d3b0e5de178" :answer-field/id #uuid "67039e40-5de3-570b-bbed-0d3b0e5de178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-94f6c867-15bb-5d02-aa0b-4ba6976af87a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b3c741e7-dfd7-5769-bd67-c9d073965e46" "answer-6fb2e354-67fd-5920-b3f1-8def0deb29e2" "answer-94f6c867-15bb-5d02-aa0b-4ba6976af87a" "answer-62649176-19b3-5dc9-882a-69ef22eb63b6" "answer-7ec24db7-f765-5318-ae89-7d5a90748c37"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3c741e7-dfd7-5769-bd67-c9d073965e46" :answer/id #uuid "b3c741e7-dfd7-5769-bd67-c9d073965e46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fb2e354-67fd-5920-b3f1-8def0deb29e2" :answer/id #uuid "6fb2e354-67fd-5920-b3f1-8def0deb29e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94f6c867-15bb-5d02-aa0b-4ba6976af87a" :answer/id #uuid "94f6c867-15bb-5d02-aa0b-4ba6976af87a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62649176-19b3-5dc9-882a-69ef22eb63b6" :answer/id #uuid "62649176-19b3-5dc9-882a-69ef22eb63b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ec24db7-f765-5318-ae89-7d5a90748c37" :answer/id #uuid "7ec24db7-f765-5318-ae89-7d5a90748c37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c255cc5-a4ec-5d1f-be58-dbb45c0a4d57" :question/id #uuid "8c255cc5-a4ec-5d1f-be58-dbb45c0a4d57" :question/math-academy-id "q-159590" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$64$** | **$68$** | **$72$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight ($y$)** | $130$ | $148$ | $164$ | $186$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and the weights, in pounds, of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-165+4.6x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=70,$ and $\\text{MSE}=3.6,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.920)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-165+4.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-165+4.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=-165.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.920)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(64-70)^{2}+(68-70)^{2}+(72-70)^{2}+(76-70)^{2} \\\\  & =36+4+4+36 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.920\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{70}^{2}}{80})}≈43.4\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=-165\\pm 43.4\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/id #uuid "fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-91bc6383-5d46-59ca-b246-89d30592f06c" "answer-c3d3d801-fda0-5c5b-82eb-e97ceb523307" "answer-8d6a69c6-a56f-5da5-a2f4-cda015f6c0cc" "answer-5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a" "answer-2629af92-040b-52b8-9b80-03dbd9c188f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91bc6383-5d46-59ca-b246-89d30592f06c" :answer/id #uuid "91bc6383-5d46-59ca-b246-89d30592f06c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-165\\pm 41.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3d3d801-fda0-5c5b-82eb-e97ceb523307" :answer/id #uuid "c3d3d801-fda0-5c5b-82eb-e97ceb523307" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-165\\pm 42.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d6a69c6-a56f-5da5-a2f4-cda015f6c0cc" :answer/id #uuid "8d6a69c6-a56f-5da5-a2f4-cda015f6c0cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-165\\pm 43.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a" :answer/id #uuid "5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-165\\pm 43.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2629af92-040b-52b8-9b80-03dbd9c188f3" :answer/id #uuid "2629af92-040b-52b8-9b80-03dbd9c188f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-165\\pm 44.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193891 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i)$ drawn from a population. \n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, we can calculate the linear regression line for this data, which we'll denote as\n\n$$\ny = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x.\n$$\n\nOur *estimates* for the **slope parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ and **intercept parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$  are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}, \\qquad \\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} -  \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ are the mean values of $x_i$ and $y_i,$ and the quantities $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are given by\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \n\\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nPlotting our regression line alongside the sample data, we might get a diagram similar to the following: \n\n![](images/68/681a96293905841b417dd7ca9faa2631aedc5ad57e4f2d95fcd40089b9e83c6e.png)\n\nNotice that we referred to $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ and $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as *estimates*. This is because if we were to conduct a census on the population, we'd be able to calculate the \"true\" regression equation for the data that considers every population member. We'll denote this \"true\" regression line as \n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x.\n$$\n\nThe two lines are shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df08670fd3c4aff99e994692eff28a49a9ec47d4aa73185682e3e0e829e3d562.png)\n\nTo summarize, \n\n- the regression equation $y = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x$ is an estimate of the true regression equation $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x,$\n\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" slope parameter $\\beta_1,$ and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" intercept parameter $\\beta_0.$\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn how to construct confidence intervals for the intercept parameter $\\beta_0.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193892 :tutorial/content "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}_{\\textrm{standard} \\ \\,\\:\\: \\textrm{error}}}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nThe derivation of this result is rather long, and we leave it until the end of the lesson. For now, note the following:\n\n- Each data point in the sample has some *residual* (i.e., error) associated with it. The error of our regression model at the data point $(x_i,y_i)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i).\n$$\n\n- The expression $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$ gives the sum of the squared residuals of our regression model.\n\n- Even considering the entire population, the data points will not fall on a perfectly straight line. Thus, errors are present even in the \"true\" regression model. These errors have some (unknown) variance, which we'll denote as $\\sigma^2.$\n\n- It can be shown that the $\\textrm{MSE}$ is an *unbiased* estimator of $\\sigma^2.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193900 :tutorial/content "We'll now derive our expression for the confidence interval for our intercept parameter estimate $\\widehat{\\beta_0}.$\n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i).$\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nLet's assume that our \"global\" regression line (i.e., the line that best fits the entire population) is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x.\n$$\n\nFor each $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ let\n\n$$\ny_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i + \\epsilon_i,\n$$\n\nwhere we assume that the error terms $\\epsilon_i$ are I.I.D, and \n\n$$\n\\epsilon_i \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nfor some unknown variance $\\sigma^2.$ The error terms and their distribution can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69135b6cdc4d34f5422da39bac5d15624d16f4817e91f282974507c711953796.png)\n\nThis means that each $y_i$ is a normally distributed random variable with\n\n$$\ny_i \\sim N\\left( \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i , \\sigma^2\\right).\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that \n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] =  \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe'll derive these results separately in a while. But for now, we'll simply state them.\n\nUsing these results, we can express the distribution of $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta}_0 \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0, \\: \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right) \\right).\n$$\n\nWe do not know $\\sigma^2,$ the variance of the error terms. However, it can be shown that the mean squared error, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2,\n$$\n\nis an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2.$\n\nOur estimate of the standard error of $\\widehat{\\beta}_0$ is now given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)} .\n$$\n\nSince this is an *estimate* of the standard error, it can be shown that the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\beta}_0 -\\beta_0}{\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] }\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom. \n\nFinally, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193893 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] = \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i.\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\overset{―}{y} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we define $k_i$ as\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{ x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}.\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} - \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite our expression for $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{0}} & =\\overset{―}{y}-{\\beta }_{1}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}}{{S}_{xx}}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to compute $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0} }]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0} }].$\n\n- For the mean, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{0}}] & =\\frac{1}{n}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}]-\\overset{―}{x}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{E}[{y}_{i}]-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{E}[{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}[{\\beta }_{0}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{x}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}[{\\beta }_{0}{\\cancel{{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}}}^{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\cancel{{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{x}_{i}}}^{1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\beta }_{0}}{n}{\\sum }_{i}1+\\frac{{\\beta }_{1}}{n}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\beta }_{0}}{n}\\cdot n+\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the variance, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{0}}] & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}]+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}]+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}n{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}n{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{S}_{xx}} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}(\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0,\\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\\right).\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192927 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186192387 :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7  x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=2.4,$ the mean square error of the model is $1.25,$ and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg) }\n$$\n\n*"] [17592186192387 :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 1.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.90=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.25 \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{8} + \\dfrac{2.4^2}{42} \\bigg)} \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  1.6 \\pm 1.1\n$$"] [17592186192388 :question/problem "| **Hours ($x$)** | **$4$** | **$4.5$** | **$5.5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grades ($y$)** | $62$ | $65$ | $77$ | $81$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours each of four students spent studying for a biology exam and the grades they obtained on the exam. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=5,$ and $\\textrm{MSE}=1.375,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg) }.\n$$"] [17592186192388 :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x= 21.3 + 10x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\beta_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=21.3 + 10x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 21.3.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.005} = 2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing $S_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(4-5)^{2}+(4.5-5)^{2}+(5.5-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0.25+0.25+1 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 2.920 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.375\\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{5^2}{2.5} \\bigg)} \\approx 11.0\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  21.3 \\pm 11.0\n$$"] ["question-f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" :question/id #uuid "f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9"] ["question-f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159563"] ["question-f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{10},{y}_{10})$ of $n=10$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2.7,$ the mean square error of the model is $3.6,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=82.5,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*"] ["question-f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.8-3.3x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{2.7}^{2}}{82.5})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.8\\pm 2.8\n$$"] ["question-f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/id #uuid "08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7"] ["answer-25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90" :answer/id #uuid "25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90"] ["answer-25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90" :answer/value "2.8\\pm 1.1"] ["answer-868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c" :answer/id #uuid "868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c"] ["answer-868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c" :answer/value "2.8\\pm 1.3"] ["answer-460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7" :answer/id #uuid "460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7"] ["answer-460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7" :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.1"] ["answer-b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a" :answer/id #uuid "b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a"] ["answer-b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a" :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.5"] ["answer-e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer/id #uuid "e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95"] ["answer-e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.8"] ["answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/correct "answer-e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95"] ["answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/choices "answer-25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90"] ["answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/choices "answer-868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c"] ["answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/choices "answer-460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7"] ["answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/choices "answer-b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a"] ["answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/choices "answer-e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95"] ["question-f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7"] [17592186192682 :knowledge-point/questions "question-f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9"] [17592186192979 :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{20},{y}_{20})$ of $n=20$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2,$ the mean square error of the model is $8,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=24,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.101)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $18$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*"] [17592186192979 :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.5+5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=3.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.101)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{18,0.025}=2.101.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.101\\cdot \\sqrt{8\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{24})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.5\\pm 2.8\n$$"] [17592186192979 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-a18bd7cb-4599-5047-a357-7726837ec776" :answer/id #uuid "a18bd7cb-4599-5047-a357-7726837ec776"] ["answer-a18bd7cb-4599-5047-a357-7726837ec776" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a18bd7cb-4599-5047-a357-7726837ec776" :answer/value "3.5\\pm 1.2"] ["answer-a9cd2e1a-8327-5ae8-8f04-0b0a204554bc" :answer/id #uuid "a9cd2e1a-8327-5ae8-8f04-0b0a204554bc"] ["answer-a9cd2e1a-8327-5ae8-8f04-0b0a204554bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a9cd2e1a-8327-5ae8-8f04-0b0a204554bc" :answer/value "3.5\\pm 2.8"] ["answer-3b4ae633-d836-59a7-8030-002829769a32" :answer/id #uuid "3b4ae633-d836-59a7-8030-0