nswer-9870a91b-b9ca-50af-8384-c34539b8a4b1"] ["answer-field-c654e4fd-8bc7-5442-8ca4-e6531d76e850" :answer-field/choices "answer-f661769d-8896-57ab-83d7-39025d585319"] ["question-24ed500c-c9f9-51ed-ab79-e8e54e985d91" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-c654e4fd-8bc7-5442-8ca4-e6531d76e850"] [17592186192682 :knowledge-point/questions "question-24ed500c-c9f9-51ed-ab79-e8e54e985d91"] ["question-812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf" :question/id #uuid "812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf"] ["question-812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-159561"] ["question-812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=1.2,$ the mean square error of the model is $0.7,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=18.9,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.228)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*"] ["question-812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.4+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.4.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.228)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{10,0.025}=2.228.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.228\\cdot \\sqrt{0.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{{1.2}^{2}}{18.9})}≈0.7\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.4\\pm 0.7\n$$"] ["question-812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/id #uuid "b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c"] ["answer-e8c71797-2619-523f-998b-4dc9f5e6a269" :answer/id #uuid "e8c71797-2619-523f-998b-4dc9f5e6a269"] ["answer-e8c71797-2619-523f-998b-4dc9f5e6a269" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e8c71797-2619-523f-998b-4dc9f5e6a269" :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.1"] ["answer-b312c9b8-2b8a-552e-98f0-214625871779" :answer/id #uuid "b312c9b8-2b8a-552e-98f0-214625871779"] ["answer-b312c9b8-2b8a-552e-98f0-214625871779" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b312c9b8-2b8a-552e-98f0-214625871779" :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.2"] ["answer-93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c" :answer/id #uuid "93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c"] ["answer-93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c" :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.7"] ["answer-489fcd75-9368-5e9b-acbf-94835400f3d5" :answer/id #uuid "489fcd75-9368-5e9b-acbf-94835400f3d5"] ["answer-489fcd75-9368-5e9b-acbf-94835400f3d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-489fcd75-9368-5e9b-acbf-94835400f3d5" :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.9"] ["answer-92b0899f-5335-5dd0-a82e-12c93b11cd39" :answer/id #uuid "92b0899f-5335-5dd0-a82e-12c93b11cd39"] ["answer-92b0899f-5335-5dd0-a82e-12c93b11cd39" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-92b0899f-5335-5dd0-a82e-12c93b11cd39" :answer/value "2.4\\pm 1.1"] ["answer-field-b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/correct "answer-93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c"] ["answer-field-b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/choices "answer-e8c71797-2619-523f-998b-4dc9f5e6a269"] ["answer-field-b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/choices "answer-b312c9b8-2b8a-552e-98f0-214625871779"] ["answer-field-b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/choices "answer-93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c"] ["answer-field-b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/choices "answer-489fcd75-9368-5e9b-acbf-94835400f3d5"] ["answer-field-b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/choices "answer-92b0899f-5335-5dd0-a82e-12c93b11cd39"] ["question-812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c"] [17592186192682 :knowledge-point/questions "question-812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf"] ["question-69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d" :question/id #uuid "69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d"] ["question-69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159557"] ["question-69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$67$** | **$70$** | **$73$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Shoe size ($y$)** | $8.5$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and corresponding shoe sizes of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=71.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.338,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.  \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.920)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$"] ["question-69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-26.6+0.5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=-26.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.920)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(67-71.5)^{2}+(70-71.5)^{2}+(73-71.5)^{2}+(76-71.5)^{2} \\\\  & =20.25+2.25+2.25+20.25 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.920\\cdot \\sqrt{0.338\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{71.5}^{2}}{45})}≈18.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=-26.6\\pm 18.1\n$$"] ["question-69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-5f3ed31d-762f-57be-a238-32e2c1426527" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3ed31d-762f-57be-a238-32e2c1426527"] ["answer-e5ebf936-7757-56b0-a68f-58f3fcbd404b" :answer/id #uuid "e5ebf936-7757-56b0-a68f-58f3fcbd404b"] ["answer-e5ebf936-7757-56b0-a68f-58f3fcbd404b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e5ebf936-7757-56b0-a68f-58f3fcbd404b" :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 10.3"] ["answer-e4f2c162-946e-59cc-8842-379aa299e7ac" :answer/id #uuid "e4f2c162-946e-59cc-8842-379aa299e7ac"] ["answer-e4f2c162-946e-59cc-8842-379aa299e7ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e4f2c162-946e-59cc-8842-379aa299e7ac" :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 16.2"] ["answer-c5f1300c-5d71-5a55-8165-193b27ab2363" :answer/id #uuid "c5f1300c-5d71-5a55-8165-193b27ab2363"] ["answer-c5f1300c-5d71-5a55-8165-193b27ab2363" :answer/type :answer.type/