llowing:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-165+4.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=-165.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.920)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(64-70)^{2}+(68-70)^{2}+(72-70)^{2}+(76-70)^{2} \\\\  & =36+4+4+36 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.920\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{70}^{2}}{80})}≈43.4\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=-165\\pm 43.4\n$$"] ["question-8c255cc5-a4ec-5d1f-be58-dbb45c0a4d57" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/id #uuid "fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a"] ["answer-91bc6383-5d46-59ca-b246-89d30592f06c" :answer/id #uuid "91bc6383-5d46-59ca-b246-89d30592f06c"] ["answer-91bc6383-5d46-59ca-b246-89d30592f06c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-91bc6383-5d46-59ca-b246-89d30592f06c" :answer/value "-165\\pm 41.7"] ["answer-c3d3d801-fda0-5c5b-82eb-e97ceb523307" :answer/id #uuid "c3d3d801-fda0-5c5b-82eb-e97ceb523307"] ["answer-c3d3d801-fda0-5c5b-82eb-e97ceb523307" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-c3d3d801-fda0-5c5b-82eb-e97ceb523307" :answer/value "-165\\pm 42.8"] ["answer-8d6a69c6-a56f-5da5-a2f4-cda015f6c0cc" :answer/id #uuid "8d6a69c6-a56f-5da5-a2f4-cda015f6c0cc"] ["answer-8d6a69c6-a56f-5da5-a2f4-cda015f6c0cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-8d6a69c6-a56f-5da5-a2f4-cda015f6c0cc" :answer/value "-165\\pm 43.2"] ["answer-5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a" :answer/id #uuid "5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a"] ["answer-5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a" :answer/value "-165\\pm 43.4"] ["answer-2629af92-040b-52b8-9b80-03dbd9c188f3" :answer/id #uuid "2629af92-040b-52b8-9b80-03dbd9c188f3"] ["answer-2629af92-040b-52b8-9b80-03dbd9c188f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-2629af92-040b-52b8-9b80-03dbd9c188f3" :answer/value "-165\\pm 44.7"] ["answer-field-fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/correct "answer-5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a"] ["answer-field-fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/choices "answer-91bc6383-5d46-59ca-b246-89d30592f06c"] ["answer-field-fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/choices "answer-c3d3d801-fda0-5c5b-82eb-e97ceb523307"] ["answer-field-fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/choices "answer-8d6a69c6-a56f-5da5-a2f4-cda015f6c0cc"] ["answer-field-fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/choices "answer-5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a"] ["answer-field-fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/choices "answer-2629af92-040b-52b8-9b80-03dbd9c188f3"] ["question-8c255cc5-a4ec-5d1f-be58-dbb45c0a4d57" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a"] [17592186192683 :knowledge-point/questions "question-8c255cc5-a4ec-5d1f-be58-dbb45c0a4d57"] [17592186193891 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i)$ drawn from a population. \n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, we can calculate the linear regression line for this data, which we'll denote as\n\n$$\ny = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x.\n$$\n\nOur *estimates* for the **slope parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ and **intercept parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$  are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}, \\qquad \\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} -  \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ are the mean values of $x_i$ and $y_i,$ and the quantities $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are given by\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \n\\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nPlotting our regression line alongside the sample data, we might get a diagram similar to the following: \n\n![](images/68/681a96293905841b417dd7ca9faa2631aedc5ad57e4f2d95fcd40089b9e83c6e.png)\n\nNotice that we referred to $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ and $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as *estimates*. This is because if we were to conduct a census on the population, we'd be able to calculate the \"true\" regression equation for the data that considers every population member. We'll denote this \"true\" regression line as \n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x.\n$$\n\nThe two lines are shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df08670fd3c4aff99e994692eff28a49a9ec47d4aa73185682e3e0e829e3d562.png)\n\nTo summarize, \n\n- the regression equation $y = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x$ is an estimate of the true regression equation $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x,$\n\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" slope parameter $\\beta_1,$ and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" intercept parameter $\\beta_0.$\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn how to construct confidence intervals for the intercept parameter $\\beta_0.$"] [17592186193892 :tutorial/content "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}_{\\textrm{standard} \\ \\,\\:\\: \\textrm{error}}}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nThe derivation of this result is rather long, and we leave it until the end of the lesson. For now, note the following:\n\n- Each data point in the sample has some *residual* (i.e., error) associated with it. The error of our regression model at the data point $(x_i,y_i)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i).\n$$\n\n- The expression $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$ gives the sum of the squared residuals of our regression model.\n\n- Even considering the entire population, the data points will not fall on a perfectly straight line. Thus, errors are present even in the \"true\" regression model. These errors have some (unknown) variance, which we'll denote as $\\sigma^2.$\n\n- It can be shown that the $\\textrm{MSE}$ is an *unbiased* estimator of $\\sigma^2.$"] [17592186193900 :tutorial/content "We'll now derive our expression for the confidence interval for our intercept parameter estimate $\\widehat{\\beta_0}.$\n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i).$\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nLet's assume that our \"global\" regression line (i.e., the line that best fits the entire population) is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x.\n$$\n\nFor each $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ let\n\n$$\ny_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i + \\epsilon_i,\n$$\n\nwhere we assume that the error terms $\\epsilon_i$ are I.I.D, and \n\n$$\n\\epsilon_i \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nfor some unknown variance $\\sigma^2.$ The error terms and their distribution can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69135b6cdc4d34f5422da39bac5d15624d16f4817e91f282974507c711953796.png)\n\nThis means that each $y_i$ is a normally distributed random variable with\n\n$$\ny_i \\sim N\\left( \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i , \\sigma^2\\right).\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that \n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] =  \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe'll derive these results separately in a while. But for now, we'll simply state them.\n\nUsing these results, we can express the distribution of $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta}_0 \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0, \\: \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right) \\right).\n$$\n\nWe do not know $\\sigma^2,$ the variance of the error terms. However, it can be shown that the mean squared error, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2,\n$$\n\nis an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2.$\n\nOur estimate of the standard error of $\\widehat{\\beta}_0$ is now given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)} .\n$$\n\nSince this is an *estimate* of the standard error, it can be shown that the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\beta}_0 -\\beta_0}{\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] }\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom. \n\nFinally, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$"] [17592186193893 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] = \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i.\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\overset{―}{y} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we define $k_i$ as\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{ x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}.\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} - \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite our expression for $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{0}} & =\\overset{―}{y}-{\\beta }_{1}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}}{{S}_{xx}}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to compute $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0} }]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0} }].$\n\n- For the mean, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{0}}] & =\\frac{1}{n}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}]-\\overset{―}{x}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{E}[{y}_{i}]-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{E}[{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}[{\\beta }_{0}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{x}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}[{\\beta }_{0}{\\cancel{{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}}}^{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\cancel{{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{x}_{i}}}^{1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\beta }_{0}}{n}{\\sum }_{i}1+\\frac{{\\beta }_{1}}{n}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\beta }_{0}}{n}\\cdot n+\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the variance, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{0}}] & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}]+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}]+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}n{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}n{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{S}_{xx}} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}(\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0,\\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [17592186192927 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0]] "retractions" [[17592186192387 :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7 x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=2.4$, the mean square error of the model is $1.25$, and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is given by $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg)}$*"] [17592186192387 :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7x\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7x$, our point estimate is\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 1.6$.\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.90=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05$.\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05$. As a result,\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.25 \\cdot \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{8} + \\dfrac{2.4^2}{42} \\bigg)} \\approx 1.1$\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = 1.6 \\pm 1.1$"] [17592186192388 :question/problem "| **Hours ($x$)** | **$4$** | **$4.5$** | **$5.5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Grades ($y$)** | $62$ | $65$ | $77$ | $81$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours each of four students spent studying for a biology exam and the grades they obtained on the exam. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=5$, and $\\textrm{MSE}=1.375$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is*\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg)}$."] [17592186192388 :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x= 21.3 + 10x\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\beta_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=21.3 + 10x$, our point estimate is\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 21.3$.\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05$.\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05$. As a result,\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.005} = 2.920$.Computing $S_{xx}$, we get\n$∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}|= (4 - 5)^{2} + (4.5 - 5)^{2} + (5.5 - 5)^{2} + (6 - 5)^{2}; = 1 + 0.25 + 0.25 + 1; = 2.5$. Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = 2.920 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.375\\cdot \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{5^2}{2.5} \\bigg)} \\approx 11.0$\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = 21.3 \\pm 11.0$"] [17592186192979 :question/problem "Let $y^(^) = β^(^)_{0} + β^(^)_{1}x = 3.5 + 5x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_{1}, y_{1}), …, (x_{20}, y_{20})$ of $n = 20$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $x^(―) = 2$, the mean square error of the model is $8$, and $S_{xx} = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} = 24$, find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.101) = 0.025$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $18$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $β^(^)_{0}$ is given by $SE[β^(^)_{0}] = \\sqrt{MSE \\cdot (\\frac{1}{n} + (x^(―)^{2})/(S_{xx}))}$*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189761] [17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189762] [17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189763] [17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189764] [17592186189760 :answer-field/choices 17592186189765] [17592186193891 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i)$ drawn from a population.\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, we can calculate the linear regression line for this data, which we'll denote as\n\n$$\ny = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x\n$$\n\nOur *estimates* for the **slope parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ and **intercept parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}, \\qquad \\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} - \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ are the mean values of $x_i$ and $y_i$, and the quantities $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are given by\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y})\n$$\n\nPlotting our regression line alongside the sample data, we might get a diagram similar to the following:\n\n![](images/68/681a96293905841b417dd7ca9faa2631aedc5ad57e4f2d95fcd40089b9e83c6e.png)\n\nNotice that we referred to $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ and $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as *estimates*. This is because if we were to conduct a census on the population, we'd be able to calculate the \"true\" regression equation for the data that considers every population member. We'll denote this \"true\" regression line as\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nThe two lines are shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df08670fd3c4aff99e994692eff28a49a9ec47d4aa73185682e3e0e829e3d562.png)\n\nTo summarize,\n\n- the regression equation $y = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x$ is an estimate of the true regression equation $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$,\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" slope parameter $\\beta_1$, and\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" intercept parameter $\\beta_0$.\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn how to construct confidence intervals for the intercept parameter $\\beta_0$."] [17592186193892 :tutorial/content "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}_{\\textrm{standard} \\ \\,\\:\\: \\textrm{error}}}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n\nThe derivation of this result is rather long, and we leave it until the end of the lesson. For now, note the following:\n\n- Each data point in the sample has some *residual* (i.e., error) associated with it. The error of our regression model at the data point $(x_i,y_i)$ is given by\n$(y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)$.\n- The expression $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$ gives the sum of the squared residuals of our regression model.\n- Even considering the entire population, the data points will not fall on a perfectly straight line. Thus, errors are present even in the \"true\" regression model. These errors have some (unknown) variance, which we'll denote as $\\sigma^2$.\n- It can be shown that the $\\textrm{MSE}$ is an *unbiased* estimator of $\\sigma^2$."] [17592186193900 :tutorial/content "We'll now derive our expression for the confidence interval for our intercept parameter estimate $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$.\n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i)$.\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nLet's assume that our \"global\" regression line (i.e., the line that best fits the entire population) is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nFor each\n\n$$\ni=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\nlet\n\n$$\ny_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i + \\epsilon_i\n$$\n\nwhere we assume that the error terms $\\epsilon_i$ are I.I.D, and\n\n$$\n\\epsilon_i \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nfor some unknown variance $\\sigma^2$. The error terms and their distribution can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69135b6cdc4d34f5422da39bac5d15624d16f4817e91f282974507c711953796.png)\n\nThis means that each $y_i$ is a normally distributed random variable with\n\n$$\ny_i \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i, \\sigma^2\\right)\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] = \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe'll derive these results separately in a while. But for now, we'll simply state them.\n\nUsing these results, we can express the distribution of $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta}_0 \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0, \\: \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right) \\right)\n$$\n\nWe do not know $\\sigma^2$, the variance of the error terms. However, it can be shown that the mean squared error, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nis an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2$.\n\nOur estimate of the standard error of $\\widehat{\\beta}_0$ is now given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nSince this is an *estimate* of the standard error, it can be shown that the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\beta}_0 -\\beta_0}{\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}]}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\nFinally, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$"] [17592186193893 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] = \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y^(―) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x^(―) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + y^(―)nx^(―) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we define $k_i$ as\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} - \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite our expression for $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nβ_{0}^(^) &= y^(―) - β_{1}x^(―) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i} - \\frac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}x^(―) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i} - (∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i})/(S_{xx})x^(―) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i} - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}y_{i}x^(―) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i} - x^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}y_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to compute $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0}}]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0}}]$.\n\n- For the mean, we have\n$E[β_{0}^(^)]|= \\frac{1}{n}E[∑_(i)y_{i}] - x^(―)E[∑_(i)k_{i}y_{i}]; = \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i)E[y_{i}] - x^(―)∑_(i)E[k_{i}y_{i}]; = \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i)(β_{0} + β_{1}x_{i}) - x^(―)∑_(i)k_{i}(β_{0} + β_{1}x_{i}); = \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i)(β_{0} + β_{1}x_{i}) - x^(―)[β_{0}∑_(i)k_{i} + β_{1}∑_(i)k_{i}x_{i}]; = \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i)(β_{0} + β_{1}x_{i}) - x^(―)[β_{0}∑_(i)k_{i}^{0} + β_{1}∑_(i)k_{i}x_{i}^{1}]; = \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i)(β_{0} + β_{1}x_{i}) - x^(―)β_{1}; = \\frac{β_{0}}{n}∑_(i)1 + \\frac{β_{1}}{n}∑_(i)x_{i} - x^(―)β_{1}; = \\frac{β_{0}}{n} \\cdot n + x^(―)β_{1} - x^(―)β_{1}; = β_{0}$.\n- For the variance, we have\n$Var[β_{0}^(^)]|= \\frac{1}{n^{2}}Var[∑_(i)y_{i}] + x^(―)^{2}Var[∑_(i)k_{i}y_{i}]; = \\frac{1}{n^{2}}∑_(i)Var[y_{i}] + x^(―)^{2}∑_(i)k_{i}^{2}Var[y_{i}]; = \\frac{1}{n^{2}}nσ^{2} + x^(―)^{2}∑_(i)k_{i}^{2}σ^{2}; = \\frac{1}{n^{2}}nσ^{2} + x^(―)^{2}σ^{2}∑_(i)k_{i}^{2}; = \\frac{1}{n}σ^{2} + x^(―)^{2}σ^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{S_{xx}}; = σ^{2}(\\frac{1}{n} + (x^(―)^{2})/(S_{xx}))$.\n\nSo finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0,\\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\\right)\n$$"]]}]